# Byte-identical checkouts on every platform.
#
# The SHA-256 values in each benchmark's checksums_sha256.txt are computed over the bytes
# stored in this repository. Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true, which
# rewrites line endings on checkout; a reviewer cloning there would then fail every
# checksum in the package while nothing was actually wrong with the data. Turning text
# conversion off for everything keeps a clone byte-for-byte identical to what was
# published, which is the only state in which those checksums mean anything.
* -text

# ...except the files a rerun rewrites.
#
# Nothing under results/ carries a published checksum -- those are taken over the
# benchmark inputs in DATA/, which the rule above keeps byte-for-byte. What
# results/ carries instead is output, rewritten on every run, and Python's text mode ends
# the lines the way the platform does: CRLF on Windows, LF everywhere else. Stored
# verbatim, that turns an unchanged number into a whole-file diff for the next person to
# rerun on the other platform, and it did. Normalizing means the bytes in the repository
# are LF whichever machine produced them, so a rerun leaves the tree clean on all of them.
results/**/*.json text eol=lf
results/**/*.csv text eol=lf
results/**/*.txt text eol=lf
results/**/*.svg text eol=lf

# Python is edited and rewritten by the maintenance scripts, and no checksum covers it.
*.py text eol=lf
# Written by the county build and by the map, and not checksummed either.
DATA/**/discovery_summary.json text eol=lf
DATA/**/*.svg text eol=lf

